TEXT 144 -112 Left 3 ;#284 Basic RF bias tee
TEXT 144 -48 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lxgpm-UXTNY
TEXT 144 0 Left 2 ;Page 1 of Alan's notes: C1 passes RF and blocks
TEXT 144 32 Left 2 ;DC, L1 passes DC and blocks RF. Ideal parts here.
TEXT 144 624 Left 2 ;Alan gives the low frequency limit as fL = 1 / (2 pi C (RL + RS)). With his formula, 10 nF
TEXT 144 656 Left 2 ;between a 50 ohm source and a 50 ohm load puts it at 159.2 kHz, and the sweep checks that.
TEXT 144 704 Left 2 ;Mid-band the source and load halve the signal, so 0.5 V out of a 1 V source is the reference
TEXT 144 736 Left 2 ;and the -3 dB point is 0.3536 V. L1 also shunts the output at low frequency, but 1 mH is
TEXT 144 768 Left 2 ;1 kohm at 159 kHz, twenty times the load, so the capacitor sets the corner as his formula assumes.
TEXT 144 816 Left 2 ;Alan's notes give no component values, so C1, L1, Vdc and the 50 ohm ports are choices. The
TEXT 144 848 Left 2 ;parts are ideal: no capacitor series inductance and no inductor self-capacitance, which is why
TEXT 144 880 Left 2 ;this file has nothing to say about the upper band edge that his page 2 is about.
TEXT 1000 160 Left 2 !.ac dec 200 1k 1G
TEXT 1000 200 Left 2 !.meas ac vmid FIND mag(V(rf_plus_dc)) AT 10Meg
TEXT 1000 240 Left 2 !.meas ac flow WHEN mag(V(rf_plus_dc))=0.353553 RISE=1
